\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We propose a controlled replication study of traffic-forecasting baselines, motivated by a source audit of GTS and Graph WaveNet. The GTS appendix corrects an earlier metric calculation, while Graph WaveNet explicitly excludes missing values during training and testing. These details show why reported values must retain their experimental context. The proposed study will align data splits, preprocessing, forecast horizons, and metric masking before evaluating models under one implementation. The present work audits published evidence and has not run forecasting experiments. All extracted numeric results remain provisional pending independent review.
\end{abstract}
\section{Related work}
The GTS paper reports original results and subsequently supplies corrected results in Appendix D. The authors explain that the earlier version calculated MAE, RMSE, and MAPE differently from the official DCRNN publication. Original and corrected tables must therefore remain distinct evidence records; a source quotation containing a number alone does not resolve this distinction. \cite{arXiv210106861}

Graph WaveNet reports results at 15, 30, and 60 minutes on METR-LA and PEMS-BAY. Its experimental section states that missing values are excluded from training and testing. This provides a stated evaluation detail that must be checked against the version and protocol of any candidate baseline. \cite{arXiv190600121}
\section{Evidence assessment}
The original GTS METR-LA 60-minute MAE of 2.99 must be read with the Appendix D correction. The corrected table reports 3.41. These are source-reported values from different calculation versions, not new measurements produced by this audit. We exclude the original calculation from the proposed common-protocol baseline set. \cite{arXiv210106861}

The software identifies provisional context matches as well as conflicts. Neither a matched dataset label nor an unverified extraction establishes complete protocol equivalence. Each candidate pair still requires review of masking, preprocessing, table version, result provenance, and horizon aggregation before it is used to justify an experiment. \cite{arXiv190600121,arXiv210106861}
